<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Steel tile inherits constructionSkillPrerequisite 3 from TileMetalBase.
  GenConstruct.CanConstruct checks this value. The job driver repeats the check on every tick.
  A pawn with construction skill 2 therefore never lays steel tile.
  Worksite.Pick selects only work that the pawn can finish, excluding more than half the errand table for such pawns.
  Their visible output consists of monuments and ruins, without paving.

  If every agent lacks the required skill, Worksite.Sweep destroys frames that none can complete.
  Even a floor frame queued by a skilled agent can disappear a second later.
  Patch_HideFloorFrames hides floor frames, so these failures are not visible.

  As with SteadyHands and Patch_AgentsCanBuild, pawn skill must not determine what the worksite can build.
  A plate represents a tenth of a second of working time.
  Paving provides visible output from a short period of work.

  This patch also removes the research prerequisite.
  Neither CanConstruct nor CanPlaceBlueprintAt checks research. The Architect menu checks it, but SlopWorld removes that menu.
  Worksite places frames directly without a designator, so the research requirement serves no purpose here.

  This operation changes base game definitions only inside a SlopWorld profile.
  Other profiles retain the original steel tile requirements.
  See Source/SlopWorld/Bootstrap/ModProfile.cs.
-->
<Patch>
  <Operation Class="SlopWorld.PatchOperationInProfile">
    <operations>
      <!--
        Add the value because the abstract parent defines it, not MetalTile itself.
        An explicit child node overrides the inherited value.
-->
      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>/Defs/TerrainDef[defName='MetalTile']</xpath>
        <value>
          <constructionSkillPrerequisite>0</constructionSkillPrerequisite>
          <researchPrerequisites Inherit="False" IsNull="True" />
        </value>
      </li>
    </operations>
  </Operation>
</Patch>
